    body: `
<p>In Arabic every noun is masculine or feminine: not only people, but a car, a school, a door. English does this only with "he" and "she". Arabic does it with "it", with "this", and with every describing word.</p>
<h3>The sign: ة</h3>
<p>Most feminine nouns end in ة, the taa marbuta: سَيَّارَة <i>sayyāra</i> "car", مَدْرَسَة <i>madrasa</i> "school". Nouns without it are usually masculine: بَيْت, كِتَاب, بَاب. When you meet a new noun, check its last letter first: it tells you which "this", which "it" and which form of every describing word to use. Write ة with its two dots; without them, ه is a different letter, a plain h.</p>
{{ex:هٰذِهِ سَيَّارَة|hādhihi sayyāra|This is a car.}}
{{ex:هٰذَا بَيْت|hādhā bayt|This is a house.}}
<h3>People</h3>
<p>Arabic always has to choose a gender for a person, so the course cues it in English: "student (a woman)". Many words for people add ة for a woman: طَالِب <i>ṭālib</i> is a man student, طَالِبَة <i>ṭāliba</i> a woman student; مُدَرِّس <i>mudarris</i> and مُدَرِّسَة <i>mudarrisa</i> work the same way.</p>
{{ex:هٰذَا طَالِب|hādhā ṭālib|This is a student. (a man)}}
{{ex:هٰذِهِ مُدَرِّسَة|hādhihi mudarrisa|This is a teacher. (a woman)}}
<h3>Feminine without ة</h3>
<p>A few words are feminine because of what they mean, with no ة: أُمّ <i>umm</i> "mother", بِنْت <i>bint</i> "girl", أُخْت <i>ukht</i> "sister". Their partners are masculine: أَب <i>ab</i> "father", وَلَد <i>walad</i> "boy", أَخ <i>akh</i> "brother". For "man" and "woman" Arabic uses two different words: رَجُل <i>rajul</i> and اِمْرَأَة <i>imraʾa</i>.</p>
{{ex:هٰذَا رَجُل|hādhā rajul|This is a man.}}
{{ex:هٰذِهِ اِمْرَأَة|hādhihi imraʾa|This is a woman.}}
<h3>"It" is he or she</h3>
<p>Arabic has no separate "it". A masculine thing is هُوَ <i>huwa</i>, a feminine thing is هِيَ <i>hiya</i>. Talking about a book, "it" is هُوَ; talking about a car, "It's new" is literally "she is new":</p>
{{ex:هِيَ جَدِيدَة|hiya jadīda|It's new. (the car)}}
<h3>Describing words agree</h3>
<p>A describing word comes after its noun and agrees with it: it adds ة after a feminine noun. Lesson 17 covers this in detail.</p>
{{ex:بِنْت صَغِيرَة|bint ṣaghīra|a small girl}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using the masculine for everything, as English has no gender. هٰذَا سَيَّارَة → هٰذِهِ سَيَّارَة: a feminine noun needs the feminine "this".</div>
`,
